<edge source="iran_economic_and_hormuz::third_party_compliance" target="russia_ukraine_war::logistics_ammo_stocks" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.7</data>
  <data key="d36">Greater third‑party compliance with sanctions and interdiction efforts directly blocks or freezes financial channels and cargo routes used for covert Iran→Russia transfers of missile components, drones and propellants, thereby reducing the rate at which Russia replenishes ammunition and critical components.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">iran_economic_and_hormuz</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="iran_economic_and_hormuz::third_party_compliance" target="us_china_tech_competitio::market_concentration" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">High compliance by third‑party traders and banks cuts off a subset of smaller or niche exporters from key markets, accelerating exits and consolidation so that AI hardware sourcing becomes concentrated among large suppliers operating within compliant jurisdictions.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">iran_economic_and_hormuz</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_tech_competitio</data>
</edge>
