</edge>
<edge source="iran_economic_and_hormuz::affected_export_volume" target="russia_ukraine_war::kinetic_strike_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">A decline in Iranian export volumes (notably crude) tightens global oil supply and pushes prices up, increasing Russian energy revenue and fiscal room to procure/produce munitions and sustain higher tempo of offensive strikes in Ukraine.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">iran_economic_and_hormuz</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="iran_economic_and_hormuz::affected_export_volume" target="treasury_bond_market_tug::long_term_bond_yield" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.7</data>
  <data key="d36">Higher export volumes from affected suppliers relieve global energy/supply constraints, lowering inflation expectations and the inflation risk premium embedded in long-term Treasury yields, so an increase in exports pushes yields down.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">iran_economic_and_hormuz</data>
  <data key="d39">treasury_bond_market_tug</data>
</edge>
<edge source="iran_economic_and_hormuz::affected_export_volume" target="us_canada_trade_spat::bilateral_trade_volume" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.7</data>
  <data key="d36">An increase in export volumes from affected suppliers (e.g., restored seaborne crude from sanctioned producers) substitutes for North American sourcing, causing US buyers to import less from Canada and reducing aggregate US‑Canada goods flows.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">iran_economic_and_hormuz</data>
  <data key="d39">us_canada_trade_spat</data>
</edge>
